# --- Direct API positive-control / fix-boundary check (not from the app UI;
# curl against the same local backend, same account, to isolate exactly what
# each header shape produces) ---
# WITHOUT moduleId header (the fix's omission in the true double-null case):
#   GET /api/v1/customer/cart/list -> HTTP 400
#   body: {"errors":[{"code":"moduleId","message":"Module id required"}]}
#   Source: Admin/app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/CartController.php:85-90
#   (NEARS-771 guard, PRE-EXISTING and INTENTIONAL: ModuleCheckMiddleware
#   excepts api/v1/customer* when the header is fully ABSENT, so this
#   controller-level "fail closed" 400 is deliberate, not a defect surfaced by
#   this ticket.)
# WITH moduleId: null (the literal string — pre-fix bug reproduction, the
# known-bad baseline from NEARS-2514's correlation_id
# 6baaaae3-3569-4d4c-a374-2f6db805075b signature):
#   GET /api/v1/customer/cart/list -> HTTP 403
#   Source: Admin/app/Http/Middleware/ModuleCheckMiddleware.php:40-49
#   ($value='null' is not numeric -> Module::where('slug','null') -> not
#   found -> 403 "not_found" — this IS the historic 403 signature this ticket
#   fixes.)
#
# Conclusion: the fix converts the failure mode from a confusing/wrong 403
# (literal 'null' looked up as a slug) to either (a) 200 with real data, the
# path actually exercised end-to-end live above, or (b) in the narrower
# fully-null edge case, a clear, PRE-EXISTING, intentional 400 validation
# error — never the old silent 403. AC1's core assertion ("no
# [FAIL] endpoint=/api/v1/customer/cart/list http_status=403 line for that
# request") holds in every path exercised.
